      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 2,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Exercise: epipolar geometry and fundamental matrix estimation\n\nTwo views of the same STATIC scene, taken from different positions (the camera must translate, not just rotate). ORB features are detected in both views and matched (brute force + Lowe ratio test); 'CV Find Fundamental Matrix' then estimates F with RANSAC. The estimation is failure-tolerant: with too few matches or a degenerate configuration it outputs found=false and a zero matrix instead of stopping the workflow. cv2.computeCorrespondEpilines turns each view's matched points into epipolar lines for the OTHER view ('whichImage' is a named dropdown), and 'CV Draw Epipolar Lines' draws them (RANSAC inliers only, up to 40): a point in one view and its epipolar line in the other view share the same color, and every epipolar line must pass through the corresponding point - if the lines miss the points, the estimate is wrong. All lines converge at the epipole: the image of the other camera's center.\n\nDebug outputs: the inlier matches side by side, the 3x3 fundamental matrix ('Inspect CV Data') and the RANSAC inlier count. The two if/else nodes route the unmodified views to the previews when found=false.\n\nTips: a scene with depth variation is required - points on a single plane (a wall, a poster) make F ill-determined; for pure camera rotation use the homography workflow instead. Lower 'reproj_threshold' (epiline distance, px) for stricter inliers; switch the detectors to SIFT for wide baselines."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
